mplementation and produce concrete artifacts (report views; published documentation). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set, and none are needed.\n42\t\n43\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n44\tBoth epics correctly carry `ai_signals` (both are Implementation type; `investigation_signals` null). `architecture_claims: 1` on both is correctly calibrated — the strategy's premises (notebook-controller activity tracking, KFP run metadata, Dashboard as surfacing layer) are corroborated and the rationales cite `dashboard.md`, `notebooks.md`, and `data-science-pipelines.md`. E002's `human_process_gates: -1` is justified (mandated technical review plus editorial review). One arguable calibration: both epics score `external_dependency: 0` while their rationales describe the no-external-dependency condition (rubric value +1). For E001 the cross-team Notebooks follow-up risk could ground a neutral 0, but the rationale does not say so; E002's stated rationale (\"no upstream or vendor coordination; content depends only on the completed console implementation\") describes the +1 condition. Minor issue — either re-score to 1 or ground the 0 in the noted cross-team risk. Signal rationales are otherwise present, substantive, and consistent with frontmatter values.\n45\t\n46\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n47\tE001 has five testable Given/When/Then ACs covering both reports, the configurable age threshold, CSV export, and passive-component validation without code changes to the data sources. E002's ACs include the rule-mandated technical-review-against-implementation AC and an explicit guard that out-of-scope capabilities (automated reclamation, chargeback) are not documented as available. No replacement/rollback AC is needed (additive feature, not a replacement); no konflux chain applies. No externally-controlled soft ACs — the \"no code changes required\" AC is a condition the epic itself verifies against a running platform. No draft-ID references to 