\n43\t| Kubeflow Pipelines | `Data Science Pipelines` | Passive (no code changes; data source) | RHOAI Pipelines |\n44\t\n45\tBoth passive components are corroborated by architecture context: the\n46\tnotebook controller tracks last activity per kernel\n47\t(`notebooks.md`), and Kubeflow Pipelines retains run completion metadata\n48\texposed via the KFP v2 REST API with pipeline runs already surfaced in the\n49\tDashboard (`data-science-pipelines.md`). Per the passive-component rule,\n50\tvalidation of both data sources becomes acceptance criteria on the Dashboard\n51\timplementation epic (the nearest active component). The different-team\n52\tvalidation exception does not fire: the RHOAI Dashboard team consumes and\n53\tvalidates both data sources as part of building the reports, and the strategy\n54\tstates no code changes in either passive component. All three components\n55\texist in the platform (architecture context files present), so no onboarding\n56\tchain is needed; the Dashboard is already Konflux-onboarded\n57\t(`dashboard.md`).\n58\t\n59\t## Investigation Epics (Step 3)\n60\t\n61\tThe strategy's open questions table is explicitly \"None\"; there are no risks,\n62\tassumptions, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs. No Investigation epics.\n63\t\n64\tOne residual detail was evaluated and classified as an implementation\n65\tdetail, not a genuine unknown: the exact interface through which the console\n66\treads notebook-controller last-activity timestamps (the culler tracks them\n67\tvia Jupyter server API probes per `notebooks.md`, and the Dashboard consumes\n68\tthe Kubernetes API per `dashboard.md`). The strategy asserts the data exists\n69\tand frames the work as surfacing it; discovering the concrete API surface is\n70\tresolved when the team starts the work. It is captured as an acceptance\n71\tcriterion and a health warning below, not an Investigation epic.\n72\t\n73\t## Epic List\n74\t\n75\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n76\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle and stale resource reports in the RHOAI web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n78\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Document the idle/stale resource reports and safe reclamation steps | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | P2 |\n79\t\n80\t## Dependency DAG\n81\t\n82\t```mermaid\n83\tgraph TD\n84\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9016-E001: Idle and stale resource reports] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9016-E002: Documentation]\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t## DAG Justification\n88\t\n89\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n90\t|---|---|---|\n91\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes the reports that the console implementation delivers; it must be written against what was actually built. |\n92\t\n93\tNo other edges. There is a single active component and a single implementing\n94\tteam, so no component/team boundary splits apply. The two reports (HLR-1,\n95\tHLR-2) are one logical change in one component by one team — both are\n96\tper-project report views in the console reading existing metadata — and are\n97\tkept in a single epic. The Dashboard is already Konflux-onboarded, so no\n98\tonboarding edges.\n99\t\n100\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n101\t\n102\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n103\t|---|---|---|---|\n104\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) from notebook-controller activity tracking. |\n105\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Stale pipeline-run report (configurable age, retained artifacts) from KFP run metadata. Same report surface and work-product type as HLR-1; splitting would fragment one logical change. |\n106\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | (bundled) CSV export is not separately schedulable: it exports the same report data and renders on the same report surfaces the P1 work builds — a separate epic would build the same surfaces twice or force a second pass over them. Bundled as acceptance criteria on the report epic. |\n107\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Substantive documentation authoring (new doc topics: report explanations and safe reclamation steps) — distinct work stream, separate docs-authoring epic. |\n108\t\n109\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n110\t\n111\t## Priority Decisions (Step 4)\n112\t\n113\t- E001 maps to HLR-1 and HLR-2 (both P1) with HLR-3 (P2) bundled because CSV\n114\t  export shares the report surfaces and data of the P1 work — it is not a\n115\t  deferrable, independently shippable surface. Priority P1.\n116\t- E002 is a docs-authoring epic. Per the docs-authoring priority exception it\n117\t  does not push priority upstream to E001; its priority derives from the\n118\t  strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal` → P2.\n119\t\n120\t## Health Warnings\n121\t\n122\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n123\t  table, no formal acceptance criteria, no NFRs, no Staff Engineer Input.\n124\t  Owners and validation points were derived from architecture context.\n125\t- **Scope trap / data-surface risk**: the strategy asserts the\n126\t  notebook-controller last-activity data \"exists; it is just not surfaced\n127\t  anywhere.\" Architecture context shows activity is tracked per kernel\n128\t  through Jupyter server API probes by the culler. If the timestamps turn\n129\t  out not to be readable through an interface the Dashboard can consume, a\n130\t  small follow-up change in the `Notebooks` component (Workbenches team)\n131\t  would be required — outside this decomposition's epic set. Watch during\n132\t  implementation of the idle-workbench report; escalate to a new strategy or\n133\t  epic if the exposure work is non-trivial.\n134\t- **Documentation team assumption**: the `Documentation` component has no\n135\t  architecture-context file, so no canonical owning team could be derived;\n136\t  E002 is assigned to team \"Documentation\" by convention. Confirm the actual\n137\t  docs owning team at planning